</div></div></div></div><div class="vc_row wpb_row vc_row-fluid"><div class="wpb_column vc_column_container vc_col-sm-12"><div class="vc_column-inner"><div class="wpb_wrapper"><a id="6a22156aad36b" name="6a22156aad36b" class="scroll-spy-anchor"></a>
<div class="robolink-step ">

		<div class="robolink-step-head"><span>Step 2</span></div>
	
			<h2>CoDrone board</h2>
	
	<div class="wpb_text_column wpb_content_element" >
		<div class="wpb_wrapper">
			<p>The first thing you may notice is the CoDrone board. It is equipped with all kinds of components and sensors so your CoDrone can fly:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>BLE Antenna and Chip: </strong>These allow your drone to communicate with your Bluetooth board so that your drone knows what to do! When you program, the signals are sent from your Bluetooth board to the Bluetooth chip on your CoDrone.</li>
<li><strong>Microcontroller: </strong>The microcontroller acts as the CoDrone&#8217;s &#8220;brain&#8221;. It receives the signals from the Bluetooth chip and executes the commands.</li>
<li><strong>Barometer, accelerometer, optical flow sensor, and IR height sensor: </strong>Sometimes your drone needs information from the environment in order to make a decision. These sensors give information on pressure, orientation, and distance from certain objects. They will be helpful in future assignments and challenges.</li>
<li><strong>Calibration button: </strong>If your drone is not flying correctly, you may need to re-calibrate it using the button. For more detailed instructions on how to calibrate, check <a href="https://archive.learn.robolink.com/lesson/calibrating-codrone/">this</a> documentation lesson.</li>
<li><strong>RGB LED: </strong>These red, green, and blue LEDs can produce over 16 million combinations using the three colors! They indicate the status of your drone but they are also customizable.</li>
</ul>

		</div>
	</div>

	<div  class="wpb_single_image wpb_content_element vc_align_center wpb_content_element">
		
		<figure class="wpb_wrapper vc_figure">
